OpenROAD: no NaN step length from the -place_ios norms

bazel-orfs carries this against OpenROAD; it is not upstreamed.

global_placement -place_ios adds the movable IO pins to nb_gcells_ as
virtual GCells. They only slide along the perimeter, so gpl leaves them
out of the step-length norms -- but it did so by summing every GCell's
squared displacement first and subtracting the IO terms afterwards.

An IO pin crosses the die while a cell moves a fraction of a site, so
the IO terms are orders of magnitude larger than the cell terms they
are added to. In float, the cell terms are lost in the rounding of the
IO sum, and the subtraction can leave a small negative residue.
std::sqrt of that residue is a NaN, gpl sees a NaN step length, and
global_placement dies with

  [ERROR GPL-0305] RePlAce diverged during gradient descent calculation,
  resulting in an invalid step length (Inf or NaN).

The fewer and smaller the movable cells relative to the pin count, the
likelier it is: a 16-instance, 46-pin mocked SRAM in bazel-orfs
(sram/sdq_17x64, megaboom_mocked) diverges on the first backtrack,
with a -nan coordinate distance against a finite gradient distance.

Skip the IO terms instead of subtracting them: getDistance takes a mask
over nb_gcells_ positions and never accumulates a skipped term, so the
norm is over the cells alone at their own magnitude. The mask is
rebuilt from io_stor_index_to_nb_index_ on each call, since callbacks
may reorder nb_gcells_. Without -place_ios the mask is empty and the
loop is the same plain sum as before, so nothing changes for a design
that does not place its pins with global placement.

Retires at a //:bump onto an OpenROAD whose gpl computes these norms
without the IO pins in them.

